{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled from 1155 to 1194 a significant figure in the Crusades and the history of the County of Tripoli. He ascended to the county upon the death of Raymond II his father inheriting a territory strategically located on the Levantine coast a vital link between Europe and the Middle East. His reign was marked by military campaigns political maneuvering and attempts to consolidate the county's power and independence.\n\n*Early Life and Accession to the County* Raymond III was born around 1120. He received a comprehensive education preparing him for a leadership role. His father Raymond II a respected Crusader died in 1155 leaving Raymond III as the Count. The county at this time faced numerous challenges including pressure from Muslim forces and rival Crusader states. Raymond III immediately focused on strengthening the county's defenses and alliances. He inherited a complex political landscape requiring skillful diplomacy and military prowess.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw extensive military activity. He engaged in numerous battles against the Ayyubid dynasty rulers of Egypt and Syria a major power in the region. He also fought against various Muslim emirs and local rulers seeking to maintain Tripoli's autonomy. He strategically used alliances with other Crusader states like the Kingdom of Jerusalem to bolster his military strength.  His military campaigns were often costly but generally successful in preserving the county's territorial integrity. He also invested in infrastructure and trade seeking to improve the economy of Tripoli.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*  Raymond III was a skilled diplomat adept at navigating the complex political landscape of the Crusader states and the surrounding Muslim world. He forged alliances with various rulers and factions often shifting his allegiances to maintain a balance of power. He engaged in negotiations with Saladin the Ayyubid sultan of Egypt seeking to secure a lasting peace and protect Tripoli's interests. These diplomatic efforts were crucial in preventing large-scale conflicts and ensuring the county's survival.  His relationships with the Templars and Hospitallers military orders also played a vital role in his defense strategy.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1194. His death marked the end of a long and eventful reign. He is remembered as a capable military leader and a shrewd diplomat who successfully defended the County of Tripoli against numerous threats. His efforts to maintain Tripoli's independence and prosperity were highly valued by his subjects and by the Crusader states.  His reign helped to shape the future of the county contributing to its unique cultural and political identity.  He left behind a stable county albeit one constantly facing external pressures a testament to his leadership and strategic vision.  His successors continued his policies for a time though the county eventually succumbed to Ayyubid rule in 1289.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -1.7221109867095947}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -3.886147169396281e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.169585604860913e-05}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.08634165674448013}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.08379924297332764}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.0001658063702052459}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.6689160474925302e-05}, {"token": "9", "logprob": -1.9430925021879375e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.05897632613778114}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.023551715537905693}, {"token